this project is not about nostalgia this  project  is about fact and it's looking at  polaroid  not only as an art not only as a  technology but also  as a business as an industry as a  visual system i thought i would start  with  a work by uh esp walker  i'll call him sam i know him as sam sb  walker  and it's called blacked out polaroid  sign 2011.  and it's a far cry from  the snappy clean pristine  polaroid logos we continue to see on our  lives  it's it comes as a bit of a shock and i  decided to talk to sam directly  he wrote me an email i asked him quickly  to give me a sort of sense of  how he came to make this particular  picture and i think it's so interesting  i'm going to read it out to you quickly  so sam writes the basic outline is that  back in 2010  2011 i'd periodically drive by the old  polaroid campus on 128 in waltham  at this point in time the company had in  effect gone under  and the campus was in a state of  dereliction and it very much felt like  the end of an era  for the medium the campus came to  represent that for me  in a very physical way and it became  necessary to capture that moment  when the first developer bought it they  didn't bother to take the sign down  they just had someone crudely black it  out with spray paint  to me it became a powerful metaphor for  the state of the company  and by extension analog photography in  general  so i knew i just had to make an image of  it my first attempt was to capture it in  one  frame you'll see that there's five  capture it in one frame  but it forced me to be too far away and  the glow  and the texture of the sign was not  apparent  so one day i decided to return with a  small ladder  and a crappy folding table so i could  raise the level of my tripod to the  height of the sign  it was an overcast morning and i  proceeded to carefully frame and capture  the sign  in five parts given the precarious  nature of setting a tripod on a table  it's a miracle it came out as well as it  did the fact that i could see the  positives and the negatives right on the  spot gave me confidence that i was  getting the result i wanted  it goes without saying but that was the  great beauty and advantage of using  polaroid so  when i first saw this picture i thought  perhaps  oh it's a an artistic uh intervention  you know that he's taken the original  polaroid sign and  painted over and so on and that  interested me much less  um as a concept when i discovered it was  a documentary picture  and yet so it's aesthetically uh  pleasing  i got very excited and and a phrase from  i've been reading about  humphrey davey the sir humphrey davy the  famous victorian scientist  and a phrase came to mind and it goes  like this every discovery  opens a new field for investigation of  facts  shows us the imperfection of our  theories  it is justly being said that the greater  the circle of light  the greater the boundary of darkness by  which it is surrounded  and it seemed almost that comfy davey  was was talking about this picture  you
 

